---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import AdoptionStack from '../components/diagrams/AdoptionStack.astro';
import AgentLoop from '../components/diagrams/AgentLoop.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
---
<Base title="About" description={`About ${SITE.name}: ${SITE.role}, writing about AI agents, AI strategy and engineering leadership.`}>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <h1 class="statement wide">
      <strong>I'm an engineering leader at Whatfix.</strong> Most of my week goes into one question: how do we make AI actually work in
      a real product, with a real team?
    </h1>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap about-grid">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Here I share what I learn doing that. How to build AI agents that hold up in production. How leaders should plan,
        staff and measure AI work. Where AI pays off in a business, and where it doesn't.
      </p>
      <p>
        I write for three groups: engineering leaders bringing AI into their teams, junior engineers building their skills,
        and founders deciding what to build. I write from the build side, so expect specifics: tools, costs, mistakes and
        the calls I'd make again.
      </p>
      <p>Based in Bangalore. Happy to talk agents, AI strategy, or engineering leadership.</p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn dark" href={SITE.links.linkedin}>Message me on LinkedIn</a>
        <a class="btn" href={SITE.links.x}>Follow @{SITE.xHandle} on X</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="panel gold side-fig">
      <AgentLoop />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap block">
    <div class="section-head"><h2>How I think about AI in a company</h2></div>
    <div class="stack-grid">
      <figure class="panel navy stack-fig"><AdoptionStack /></figure>
      <div>
        <p class="statement small"><strong>The model is the bottom layer,</strong> not the whole stack.</p>
        <p class="muted">
          Teams often pick a model, ship a demo and stop. The layers above it decide whether anyone uses it: what data and
          tools the AI can reach, how you know it works, where it shows up in the product, and who owns it. The top layer,
          people and process, is where leaders earn their keep.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap block">
    <div class="section-head"><h2>Who I write for</h2></div>
    <ol class="audiences">
      <li><span class="mono num">01</span><h3>Engineering leaders</h3><p class="muted">How to bring AI into teams, roadmaps and hiring without the hype.</p></li>
      <li><span class="mono num">02</span><h3>Junior engineers</h3><p class="muted">Practical skills: building agents, which tools matter, how to grow a career with AI.</p></li>
      <li><span class="mono num">03</span><h3>Founders</h3><p class="muted">Where AI actually pays off in a business, and what it costs to build.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero { padding-top: 4.5rem; padding-bottom: 3rem; }
  .about-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.2fr 1fr; gap: 3rem; align-items: start; }
  .side-fig { margin: 0; padding: 8%; }
  .block { padding-top: 6rem; }
  .stack-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 3rem; align-items: center; }
  .stack-fig { margin: 0; padding: 8%; }
  .statement.small { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.8vw, 2.2rem); margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
  .audiences { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 2rem; }
  .audiences .num { color: var(--gold); display: block; margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  .audiences h3 { margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .about-grid, .stack-grid, .audiences { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
